  { name: 'Nesting',             url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Where does the Brown-necked Raven nest?', a: 'The Brown-necked Raven nests on cliff faces, rocky outcrops, and tall trees — including date palms and large acacia or eucalyptus where available. It is a flexible nester compared to strictly cliff-dependent species like the Fan-tailed Raven, using whatever elevated, secure structure is available across its desert and semi-desert range from the Sahara through the Arabian Peninsula to Central Asia.' },
  { q: 'When does the Brown-necked Raven breed?', a: 'Breeding season varies across its enormous range. In North Africa (Morocco, Algeria, Libya, Egypt), breeding is documented from approximately February to May. In the Arabian Peninsula, January to April. In Central Asia (Kazakhstan, Uzbekistan), somewhat later — March to May. The timing reflects local climate: breeding begins in the cooler, less harsh part of the year before the most extreme summer heat.' },
  { q: 'How is the Brown-necked Raven different from the Common Raven?', a: 'The Brown-necked Raven (Corvus ruficollis) is smaller than the Common Raven, with brown-tinged neck and breast feathers visible at close range. It is desert-adapted, with physiological tolerance for extreme heat and aridity. Its bill is proportionately slimmer than the Common Raven. The Common Raven is absent from most of the Brown-necked Raven\'s range, so confusion is mainly possible in the Canary Islands and Morocco where ranges approach.' },
];
