    <h1>Brown-necked Raven Nesting: Breeding and Nest Sites</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Brown-necked Raven (<em>Corvus ruficollis</em>) nests on cliff faces, rocky outcrops, and tall trees (including date palms and large acacias) across its Saharan, Middle Eastern, and Central Asian range. Clutch: typically 3–5 eggs. Breeding season: approximately February–May in North Africa; January–April in Arabia.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Nest Sites</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Brown-necked Raven is a flexible nester across an enormous range:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cliff sites:</strong> ledges and crevices on rocky outcrops, escarpments, and cliff faces in desert terrain — particularly in wadis (dry riverbeds with exposed rock faces).</li>
      <li><strong>Tree sites:</strong> tall date palms, large acacias, or planted eucalypts where cliffs are absent. This flexibility allows the species to colonise flat desert terrain where cliff-nesting ravens would not persist.</li>
      <li><strong>Human structures:</strong> occasionally on pylons, radio masts, and large buildings in areas with sparse natural nest sites.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Pairs hold year-round territories and show site fidelity, returning to the same nest site and enlarging the existing structure annually.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Nest Structure and Eggs</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The nest is a large, bulky structure of sticks and branches — substantial enough to withstand desert winds. It is lined with wool, hair, plant fibres, and occasionally cloth in areas near human settlement. Clutch: typically 3–5 eggs, pale blue-green to greenish-white, heavily blotched with brown and grey. Incubation: approximately 20–21 days, mainly by the female. Chicks fledge at approximately 5–6 weeks; post-fledging family groups remain together for several weeks before young birds become independent.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Breeding Season</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Breeding timing varies across the species' vast range from the Sahara to Central Asia:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>North Africa (Morocco, Algeria, Libya, Egypt):</strong> February–May — the cooler, relatively wetter spring.</li>
      <li><strong>Arabian Peninsula (Saudi Arabia, Yemen, Oman):</strong> January–April — breeding well before the extreme summer heat.</li>
      <li><strong>Iran, Afghanistan, Central Asia:</strong> March–May — the continental spring.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Breeding early in the year maximises the period of cooler weather during the demanding stages of chick-rearing, reducing heat stress on nestlings in desert environments.</p>
  </section>

